From e800f25242d5fbb0ea9179651b1124614c9d0615 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Ewen <ryan.ewen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 12:40:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] reducers: mirror the idempotent turnStarted guard in
 every client

The Go, Rust, Swift, Kotlin and .NET chat reducers now return the state
unchanged when chat/turnStarted names the active turn or a turn already in
the completed list, matching the TypeScript reducer. The in-place reducers
report ReduceOutcome NoOp for that branch, which is what they already use
for an action that is recognised but changes nothing.

The same one-line comment now sits above the guard in all six reducers,
including the TypeScript one, so the reason for the early exit travels
with each port.

Kotlin passed CI without the guard only because Gradle served the test
task from its build cache: the fixture directory reaches the tests as a
system property path rather than a declared task input, so the two new
fixtures never invalidated the cached result. Touching Reducers.kt makes
the test task run again.

Verified locally with the .NET fixture suite (274 of 274 pass, including
fixtures 272 and 273), dotnet format whitespace, eslint on the TypeScript
reducer, and the TypeScript reducer fixture tests. The Go, Rust, Swift and
Kotlin toolchains are not installed here, so those rely on CI.
